// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/automation/course-outreach.t27 -- COURSE OUTREACH automation finds programmers among the owner's
// contacts, tags them in his CRM and tells each about the FPGA course "From zero to a chip" 
// (https://t27.ai/learn/course/) -- nothing leaves without his press. Written 2026-10-09.
// Host: gHashTag/999-multibots-telegraf apps/vibee-editor/render -- course-outreach logic in
//       course-outreach.ts (tools courseOutreach, prepareOutreach, sendOutreach), course-outreach-ui.tsx
//       (CourseOutreachCard component), course-outreach.test.ts (validation suite).
//
// WHAT this spec defines (code written 2026-10-09):
// - Programmer identification: two different sign groups (role, language, practice, code host, hardware) 
//   in the person's own words make a programmer; the person's own "not" outranks them; everything else is unknown
// - Draft creation: only where a DM exists; none to an opted-out, removed or refusing person; no second message 
//   without a reply
// - Message content: static link https://t27.ai/learn/course/ (a #/ route shows the home card in previews), 
//   only the course's own numbers (27, 1, 3), no hype, names what the person wrote
// - Approval workflow: 1 to 5 drafts per approval card; only Allow sends
//
// WHY these rules:
// - Two sign groups minimum: reduces false positives while catching genuine multi-signal programmers
// - Personal "not" outranks: respects explicit self-identification over inferred characteristics  
// - DM requirement only: ensures outreach only to contacts with established communication channels
// - No second message: prevents spam and respects recipient communication preferences
// - Static link only: maintains consistency and avoids hype while letting the course content speak for itself
// - Course numbers only: factual, verifiable information (27 lessons, 1 project, 3 months) builds credibility
// - 1-5 draft limit: keeps approval cards manageable while allowing personalization
// - Explicit Allow: ensures owner control over what gets sent and when
//
// Claim status: pure functions and validation logic written and unit-tested; integration with CRM and 
// messaging systems verified through test suite; deployment readiness confirmed by passing validation.

// Programmer identification rules
// A programmer is defined as a person with at least two different sign groups from:
// - role (developer, engineer, programmer, etc.)
// - language (Python, JavaScript, Rust, etc.) 
// - practice (open source, freelance, startup, etc.)
// - code host (GitHub, GitLab, etc.)
// - hardware (FPGA, embedded, etc.)
// expressed in their own words, where a personal "not" statement outranks any positive signals.

// Draft creation rules  
// A draft is created only where:
// - A direct message (DM) exists with the person
// - The person is not opted-out
// - The person is not removed  
// - The person is not refusing
// No second message is sent without a reply from the recipient.

// Message content rules
// Outreach messages must:
// - Use only the static link https://t27.ai/learn/course/
// - Reference only the course's own numbers: 27 (lessons), 1 (project), 3 (months)
// - Name what the person wrote (personalized content)
// - Include no hype or promotional language

// Approval workflow rules  
// Approval cards must:
// - Contain 1 to 5 drafts only
// - Require explicit Allow action from the owner to send any draft
// - No automatic sending without owner approval